11. GLOBAL UX RULES

Apply these everywhere:

Minimum touch target: 44x44 pt; prefer 48x48 for primary touch controls.

Respect safe areas on iOS and Android.

No critical control may sit behind the home indicator, status bar, keyboard, or camera cutout.

Android hardware/back gesture must behave correctly.

No screen should require precision tapping on tiny icons.

Every destructive action that could lose meaningful work should either be reversible by undo or confirmed.

Do not spam confirmation dialogs for reversible actions.

Inputs must avoid being hidden by the keyboard.

Text should survive small phone widths and reasonable dynamic font scaling.

Use concise user copy, not engineering terminology.

Do not show raw errors to consumers.

All async actions need clear loading/success/error states.

Offline core design functionality must continue to work.

Camera permissions are requested contextually when Try-On is used, not unnecessarily on first launch.

If the user denies permission, show a proper explanation and a route to system settings.

If a feature genuinely is not supported, say so cleanly; do not silently fake it.

Never expose "simulation" messaging in a production UI.

