36. AR TRY-ON — SECOND CORE PILLAR

The AR experience must move from prototype/debug state to a consumer-ready feature.

The current shared placement logic is valuable; keep it if sound.

The current web tracker using MediaPipe is useful. The native stub must be replaced for a real mobile MVP.

36.1 Native tracker requirement

Research and choose a current, maintained on-device hand-landmark stack compatible with the actual Expo/React Native versions in the repository.

Likely architecture may involve:

a development build rather than Expo Go,

VisionCamera/frame processors or an equivalent current camera pipeline,

MediaPipe hand landmark detection or another reliable on-device landmark model,

a small native/custom Expo module if necessary.

Do not blindly install an abandoned hand-tracking plugin.

Do not upgrade the entire Expo stack only because the latest version exists. Upgrade only if required for compatibility/security and do it intentionally with validation.

The native tracker should expose a clean platform-independent structure approximately containing:

handedness

confidence

21 normalized landmarks

timestamp

Then feed those landmarks into shared placement logic.

36.2 Finger anchors

Use anatomically appropriate pairs, such as fingertip plus adjacent distal joint, to determine:

nail position

finger direction/rotation

approximate scale

Typical MediaPipe hand points:

thumb tip 4 / IP 3

index tip 8 / DIP 7

middle tip 12 / DIP 11

ring tip 16 / DIP 15

pinky tip 20 / DIP 19

Verify actual coordinate conventions rather than blindly copying these numbers.

36.3 Handedness and mirroring

Correctly handle:

left hand vs right hand

front camera mirroring

rear camera orientation

device rotation/orientation assumptions

Do not let designs swap hands or mirror text/art incorrectly.

36.4 Smoothing

Raw landmark tracking will jitter. Implement smoothing.

Use a suitable approach such as:

exponential moving average

adaptive One Euro filter

another low-latency stable filter

Goals:

overlay feels attached to nail

minimal visible jitter

not so much smoothing that the nail visibly trails the finger

Keep existing user-facing smoothing setting only if it genuinely improves use. Otherwise choose a good default and hide technical tuning.

36.5 Confidence/lost tracking behavior

When confidence falls:

do not teleport nails randomly

briefly hold/interpolate previous position

fade gracefully if tracking is lost

hide overlays after a short timeout if no reliable hand exists

show a subtle "Show your hand" hint if needed

Do not flash giant error pills during normal hand movement.

36.6 One/two hands

At minimum, one hand with all five nails must be excellent.

If two-hand detection is supported reliably, overlay both hands using the user's L/R designs.

If two hands are unstable, prioritize one-hand quality and allow a simple Left/Right hand selector for MVP.

36.7 Per-finger design

AR must read the same hands.L[5] / hands.R[5] design state as the builder.

No separate AR-only copy of the nail art.

Changing an index finger in the builder must cause the index finger AR overlay to use that nail.

36.8 AR fit/calibration

The current global/per-nail fit store is a good foundation, but expose it elegantly.

Normal try-on should work without opening calibration.

Provide a small "Adjust Fit" action that can reveal:

overall size

position toward tip/cuticle

sideways offset

small rotation

Allow per-finger adjustment only in an advanced subview.

Persist fit settings locally.

Offer Reset.

36.9 AR visual rendering

Nails must look like nails attached to a hand, not flat stickers.

Improve:

contact shadow

highlight/gloss

shape scale

rotation alignment

slight depth/offset placement

clipping/edges

If perspective/warping can be added reliably with the chosen rendering stack, do it. If not, excellent 2D transforms are preferable to unstable fake perspective.

36.10 Performance

Target:

camera preview smooth enough to feel live

tracking roughly 15–30 FPS if that is the practical model rate

overlay rendered smoothly between detections

no React re-render of the entire screen for every landmark frame if avoidable

Use worklets/shared values/native animation state where appropriate.

Resize/process camera frames at the smallest resolution that maintains reliable tracking.

36.11 AR UI

AR screen should be camera-first.

Suggested composition:

Top:

Back

small current design name

camera switch if applicable

subtle help/fit action

Center:

camera + nail overlay

very subtle detection guidance when needed

Bottom:

small design/set carousel

shutter/capture button

Edit action returning to builder

optional hand selector if only one hand at a time

Do not show debug coordinates, tracker internals, or technical calibration by default.

36.12 Permissions

Camera permission flow:

ask only on Try On

clear reason

denial state with Retry/Open Settings

restricted/unavailable state

no infinite permission loop

Photo-library permission only when saving media.

36.13 Privacy

Hand tracking should be on-device for MVP unless explicitly architected otherwise.

Do not upload camera frames to a server simply for AR tracking.

State this accurately in Help/Privacy UI if you mention it.

36.14 AR capture

Verify on a physical/development device whether react-native-view-shot actually captures the camera preview plus overlay on each platform.

If it does not, do not ship a fake capture.

Implement a reliable composition approach appropriate to the stack. This may mean:

taking a real camera photo,

applying the current nail transforms/art to a composited result,

saving/sharing that composite.

If a new graphics dependency is required solely for reliable capture, justify it and validate app-size/performance impact.

37. AR FALLBACKS

In a production build:

never label a fake background as AR,

never silently show simulated hand landmarks.

For unsupported platforms/dev web previews:

use real webcam tracking where supported,

otherwise show a clear unavailable/demo message outside the production path.

Keep simulation tools only behind an explicit developer flag if they are valuable for development.

